knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4,
                      message = FALSE)
```

## Two kinds of abstainers

In a model of vote choice the base alternative, abstention, holds two kinds
of people: *routine* nonvoters, who would not have voted whatever the
campaign did, and *occasional* nonvoters, who weighed the candidates and
stayed home this time. A standard multinomial logit treats them as one group
and, when the mixture is real, misstates how the covariates move people
between abstention and the candidates. The baseline-inflated multinomial
logit (BIMNL) of Bagozzi and Marchetti (2017) adds a split equation that
sorts cases into a regime where the base alternative is observed with
certainty and a regime where the multinomial choice operates:

\[
P(s_i = 1 \mid z_i) = \phi_i = F(z_i'\delta), \qquad
P(y_i = b) = (1 - \phi_i) + \phi_i P_{ib}, \qquad
P(y_i = j) = \phi_i P_{ij} \ (j \ne b),
\]

with \(F\) logistic (the default) or standard normal and \(P_{ij}\) the
multinomial logit probabilities. The same structure fits any alternative that
mixes two populations: `inflate` names it (default: the base).

In the discrete-choice literature, with the logistic split equation this is
the parametrized logit captivity model of Swait and Ben-Akiva (1987) with
captivity to the inflated alternative only. Their model generalizes the dogit
of Gaudry and Dagenais (1979), which adds to the multinomial logit a share of
decision makers who are captive to an alternative, by letting the captivity
odds depend on covariates. Here the captives of the inflated alternative are
the routine nonvoters, their share is \(1 - \phi_i\), and their odds are
\(\theta_i = (1 - \phi_i)/\phi_i = e^{-z_i'\delta}\).

## Fitting the model

The formula has two parts: the outcome equation and, after `|`, the split
equation. `imnl()` fits by maximum likelihood with a multistart because the
mixture likelihood can have several local maxima (see below).

```{r fit}
library(polychoice)
data(pa2006)
f <- vote ~ canvass + phone + democrat + republican + canvass:democrat + canvass:republican +
  phone:democrat + phone:republican + household + age + female + voted2004 + district156
m0 <- mnl(f, data = pa2006)
m1 <- imnl(vote ~ canvass + phone + democrat + republican + canvass:democrat +
             canvass:republican + phone:democrat + phone:republican + household + age +
             female + voted2004 + district156 |
             household + age + female + voted2004 + district156, data = pa2006)
summary(m1)
```

The summary shows the outcome equations (`alternative:term`), the split
equation (`infl_term`, the coefficients of \(z_i'\delta\) on the scale of the
link), the observed share of the inflated alternative, and the mean fitted
probability of the choice regime. The sign convention of the split
equation: \(\delta\) models \(P(s_i = 1)\), the probability of being in the
*choice* regime, so the negative `infl_age` printed above means that older
respondents are less likely to be in the choice regime, that is, more likely
to be routine abstainers. The estimates agree with the published Table A.3
of Bagozzi and Marchetti (2017) to within 0.002 in the coefficients and
standard errors that the package's tests compare (for example 1.564 (0.557)
for the Republican/other canvassing coefficient against the published 1.566
(0.558); the package's tests pin a selection of them at tolerances of one to
ten percent), and the published
log-likelihoods of both models to their two printed decimals.

## Is the split equation needed?

The plain multinomial logit is the special case \(\phi_i = 1\), a boundary of
the parameter space, so the likelihood-ratio test does not have its usual
distribution (and the split-equation slopes are not identified under the
null, so no simple correction exists; a split with an intercept only is the
exception, below under "Tests for captive choosers"). The literature compares the two models
with the Vuong (1989) test, the information criteria, and the classification
tables: `compare_models()` lines up the information criteria, `vuong()` and
`classification()` give the other two.

```{r compare}
compare_models(MNL = m0, BIMNL = m1)
vuong(m1, m0)
```

A positive Vuong statistic favours the first model; the three rows apply no
correction, the AIC correction, and the BIC correction for the number of
parameters. The normal reference of the Vuong test does not hold for
models nested at a boundary (Wilson 2015), so the calibrated comparison is the parametric bootstrap under the multinomial logit,
`boot_lr_test(m0, m1)`, which refits both models on data simulated from the
MNL and reports bootstrap p-values for the likelihood-ratio and Vuong
statistics. It is slow: every replicate refits the inflated model with its
whole search (about 40 seconds on these data), so the default `B = 199`
takes more than two hours on one core, and `cores`
spreads the replicates over workers; it is not run here.

## Quantities of interest by stage

Predictions can be made for the observed outcome (`type = "prob"`), for the
multinomial stage alone (`type = "prob_outcome"`, the probabilities among
cases in the choice regime), and for the regime itself (`type = "regime"`,
the probability of being in the choice regime). The labels `infl_` and
`"inflation"` (the `block` of `summary()`, the `component` of `tidy()`, and
`stage = "inflation"` in `first_difference()`) all mean the split equation.

```{r predict}
head(predict(m1, type = "regime"), 3)
head(predict(m1, type = "prob_outcome"), 3)
```

`first_difference()` has the same `type` switch (the change in the observed
probabilities, in the outcome-stage probabilities, or in the regime
probability) and a `stage` argument that says through which equations the
covariate is allowed to move: both (default), the outcome equation only, or
the split equation only. A covariate that appears in both equations, such
as `age` here, can thus be decomposed.

```{r fd}
first_difference(m1, "canvass", from = 0, to = 1)
first_difference(m1, "age", from = 30, to = 60, type = "regime")
first_difference(m1, "age", from = 30, to = 60, stage = "outcome")
```

## Local maxima

Mixture likelihoods are not globally concave. On `pa2006` and on `pres2004`
a search from 200 starts finds two interior maxima of the BIMNL likelihood.
The fit screens fourteen starting
values and polishes every one of them. It then challenges the incumbent with
rescaled split slopes. Each point where these searches end is classified by
the rules of the fit-quality flags: a local maximum, a boundary (diverging
coefficients, or a split equation that puts every case in one regime), a
saddle, or not converged. `m1$search` lists the distinct points, and the
number of distinct local maxima is reported, with a message when there is
more than one; the highest found need not be the global maximum. Then
`crosscheck = TRUE` runs the same search under the other split link and polishes
the fit under its own link from the rescaled estimates (which about doubles the time), and
`start` probes other basins (a row of `m1$search_theta` refits that point).

```{r modes}
m1$n_modes
m1$search
```

A fit that reports more than one mode is still the highest maximum found;
`imnl()` says so in a message (hidden in this vignette's output), so that no
analysis rests on a lower maximum unknowingly. Changing
the split-equation covariates can change the number and location of the
maxima.

## The second application: the 2004 presidential vote

`pres2004` holds the Campbell and Monson (2008) respondents of the 2004
Election Panel Study (Replication II of Bagozzi and Marchetti 2017).

```{r pres}
data(pres2004)
f2 <- vote ~ educ + female + black + hispanic + pid7 + bush_values + battleground + south + gmb +
  agegroup + evangelical + evangelical:gmb + catholic + catholic:gmb + other_religion +
  secular + secular:gmb + ideology + mobilization
p0 <- mnl(f2, data = pres2004)
p1 <- imnl(vote ~ educ + female + black + hispanic + pid7 + bush_values + battleground + south +
             gmb + agegroup + evangelical + evangelical:gmb + catholic + catholic:gmb +
             other_religion + secular + secular:gmb + ideology + mobilization |
             educ + agegroup + mobilization + secular, data = pres2004)
compare_models(MNL = p0, BIMNL = p1)
vuong(p1, p0)
```

The log-likelihoods of the published Table A.4 reproduce: the inflated
model's -545.279 agrees with the published -545.27 when truncated to its
printed digits, and a search from 200 starts finds no interior maximum above
it. So do its split-equation coefficients and the outcome-stage
coefficients the package's tests check. Two of the search's starts end
higher, at points where the Kerry and Bush coefficients diverge together
(the higher at -542.851): the outcome stage predicts 21 abstainers with
certainty, and the split equation puts the other 84 in the captive regime.
There the likelihood rises towards a supremum at infinity, a separation
within the mixture that the multinomial logit's data do not show (see `?imnl`). The
multistart does not reach that region, and the published fit is the highest
interior maximum. The raw Vuong statistic computed here is 1.68 (positive values
favour the first model, the inflated one; two-sided p = 0.09): it leans
towards the inflated model without being decisive. Its BIC-corrected version,
which charges the inflated model for its extra parameters, is -2.00 and
leans the other way, and the information criteria disagree with each other,
as in the published analysis, which reports mixed support for inflation in
these data. Which row to read: the raw statistic is Vuong's, the AIC and BIC
rows are his corrections for the difference in parameters; but since the
multinomial logit is the boundary case of the inflated model, none of them has a valid
normal reference here, and `boot_lr_test(p0, p1)` gives the calibrated
p-values.

## Tests for captive choosers

Whether the multinomial logit leaves room for routine choosers can be asked
before any inflated model is fit. `captivity_test()` is the score test of
the multinomial logit against the dogit with a constant captive share for each
alternative, computed from the multinomial logit fit alone: its joint statistic is the
Lagrange multiplier test of Tse (1987), and the one-sided statistic for each
alternative is added here. The score of a case for
alternative \(j\) is \(1\{y_i = j\}/P_{ij} - 1\): it grows when the cases
that chose \(j\) are ones the multinomial logit thought unlikely to, which is what
captive choosers produce. Each alternative gets a one-sided z statistic
(captive shares cannot be negative), and a joint test follows.

```{r captivity}
captivity_test(m0)
```

Because the multinomial logit is an ordinary boundary point of the dogit's captivity
parameters, the score test is regular there, unlike the comparison with the
inflated fit. Its weights \(1/P_{ij}\) make it heavy-tailed when some fitted
probabilities are small; `boot_pvalue(m0, captivity_test)` calibrates it by
simulation (not run here). The same weights cost it power, and here it does
not reject although the inflated multinomial logit with a constant captive share of
abstention, its own alternative, raises the log-likelihood by 10.5 on one
parameter. The score of abstention is 37.3, but its expected information
(before the coefficients are partialled out) is
10,186, half of it from one respondent whose fitted probability of abstaining
is 0.0002 and who did not abstain, and along the captive share the
log-likelihood is convex at zero, so the gain lies away from the null, where
a local test does not look. A small statistic is no evidence against
captivity. The likelihood-ratio form measures the gain itself: it fits the
inflated multinomial logit with an intercept-only split for each requested alternative
and refers the likelihood ratio to the 50:50 mixture of chi-square(0) and
chi-square(1), since a captive share of zero is a regular boundary point
when the alternative's fitted probabilities vary in a way its constants and
slopes cannot absorb (otherwise the share is not identified, and the test
says so; one refit per alternative):

```{r captivity-lr}
captivity_test(m0, alternatives = "abstain", type = "lr")
```

A split equation with covariates, as in Replication I, is not of this kind
(its slopes are not identified at the boundary), and `boot_lr_test()`
calibrates that comparison.

## Several inflated alternatives

`inflate` can name several alternatives. A case is then either in the choice
regime, where the multinomial logit operates, or captive to one of the
inflated alternatives, with the regime probabilities given by a multinomial
logit in the split covariates: the parametrized logit captivity model with a
captive share for each inflated alternative. In vote choice this holds routine
nonvoters and party loyalists in one model. Each inflated alternative gets
its own split coefficients, `infl_<alternative>:<term>`, the log-odds of
choosing against captivity to that alternative (positive: less captivity).

```{r captive}
set.seed(1)
dc <- rmnl(2000, beta = cbind(b = c(0.5, -1, 0.8), c = c(-0.3, 0.6, -0.5), d = c(0.2, 0.4, 0.3)),
           delta = cbind(a = c(1, 0.8), c = c(1.8, -0.7)), inflate = c("a", "c"))
mc <- imnl(y ~ x1 + x2 | z1, data = dc, inflate = c("a", "c"))
summary(mc)$coefficients[grep("^infl_", names(coef(mc))), ]
head(predict(mc, type = "regime"), 3)
```

The split coefficients lie within 1.5 standard errors of the values used to
simulate them (1 and 0.8 for `a`, 1.8 and -0.7 for `c`). The likelihood can
also peak near the limit in which the choice regime never picks `a`, with
every choice of `a` ascribed to captivity. In a scan of 20 samples of this
design one did (seed 9):

* the outcome constants ran to about 10, with standard errors above 13;
* the split constant of `a` fell to 0.34, with a standard error of 0.065,
  against its true value of 1.

That is the data's own maximum: a refit from the true values reaches it, and
one from the outcome constants raised by 5 returns to it. The fit raises no
flag. Large outcome constants with larger standard errors are the sign. At
such a fit the split coefficients' small standard errors are the misleading
part, and the bootstrap does not repair them: 200 refits give the split
constant of `a` a standard error of 0.084, against the analytic 0.065, for
an estimate 0.34 whose true value is 1, although they put the outcome
constants' standard errors above 300. Compare instead with the model that
has fewer inflated alternatives, by `boot_lr_test()`.

The model with fewer inflated alternatives is the boundary at which a
captive share is zero, so `boot_lr_test(imnl(y ~ x1 + x2 | z1, data = dc,
inflate = "a"), mc)` compares the two by simulation, as it compares the
inflated model with the multinomial logit.

The split covariate here, `z1`, is not in the outcome equations. In the
simulation reported in `?imnl`, splitting on an outcome covariate instead
left the intervals of that covariate's outcome coefficients at 0.89-0.90
coverage with 5,000 cases and 0.80-0.81 with 2,000.

## Links and boundaries

`link = "probit"` replaces the logistic split equation by a standard normal
one (with one inflated alternative). When the split equation puts every case in one regime the inflated model
collapses onto the multinomial logit, and with several inflated alternatives
a captive share can collapse to zero; the fit then warns that it is at a
boundary, and the standard errors of the split coefficients are not to be
interpreted.

## References

Bagozzi, B.E. and Marchetti, K. (2017). Distinguishing occasional abstention
from routine indifference in models of vote choice. *Political Science
Research and Methods*, 5(2), 277-294.

Campbell, D.E. and Monson, J.Q. (2008). The religion card: Gay marriage and
the 2004 presidential election. *Public Opinion Quarterly*, 72, 399-419.

Gaudry, M.J.I. and Dagenais, M.G. (1979). The dogit model. *Transportation
Research Part B*, 13, 105-111.

Swait, J. and Ben-Akiva, M. (1987). Empirical test of a constrained choice
discrete model: Mode choice in Sao Paulo, Brazil. *Transportation Research
Part B*, 21, 103-115.

Tse, Y.K. (1987). A diagnostic test for the multinomial logit model.
*Journal of Business and Economic Statistics*, 5, 283-286.

Vuong, Q.H. (1989). Likelihood ratio tests for model selection and
non-nested hypotheses. *Econometrica*, 57, 307-333.

Wilson, P. (2015). The misuse of the Vuong test for non-nested models to test
for zero-inflation. *Economics Letters*, 127, 51-53.
